WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.851
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.151 --> 00:00:09.002
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:15.878
Unit twenty: Coaching and Mentoring. Today you will advise formally with the subjunctive after suggest / recommend.

4
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:18.900
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.169
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:24.832
Coaching.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:30.069
Coaching means structured support to improve specific skills or performance.

8
00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:33.914
Executive coaching helped him handle difficult meetings.

9
00:00:34.517 --> 00:00:37.099
Your turn. Say: Coaching.

10
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:40.679
Coaching.

11
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:42.371
Mentor.

12
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:46.254
Mentor means an experienced and trusted adviser.

13
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.627
My mentor taught me to ask better questions.

14
00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:52.660
Your turn. Say: Mentor.

15
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.489
Mentor.

16
00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.228
Mentee.

17
00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:02.052
Mentee means a person who is guided by a mentor.

18
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.870
Each mentee meets their mentor monthly.

19
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:07.869
Your turn. Say: Mentee.

20
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:11.502
Mentee.

21
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.030
Growth.

22
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:17.804
Growth means the process of developing personally or professionally.

23
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:20.114
Feedback is the engine of growth.

24
00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:22.800
Your turn. Say: Growth.

25
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:26.714
Growth.

26
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:28.289
Feedback.

27
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:32.688
Feedback means information about performance used for improvement.

28
00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:36.351
Specific feedback is more useful than general praise.

29
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.498
Your turn. Say: Feedback.

30
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:43.392
Feedback.

31
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:45.256
Potential.

32
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.817
Potential means qualities that may develop into future success.

33
00:01:50.417 --> 00:01:53.357
A good mentor sees potential before the mentee does.

34
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.556
Your turn. Say: Potential.

35
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.413
Potential.

36
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:02.198
Reflection.

37
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:07.029
Reflection means careful thought about one's experiences and actions.

38
00:02:07.617 --> 00:02:09.790
Weekly reflection deepened her learning.

39
00:02:10.383 --> 00:02:13.570
Your turn. Say: Reflection.

40
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:17.504
Reflection.

41
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:19.019
Trust.

42
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:23.723
Trust means confidence that allows honest, open communication.

43
00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:27.431
Mentoring works only when trust is established.

44
00:02:28.017 --> 00:02:30.506
Your turn. Say: Trust.

45
00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:34.170
Trust.

46
00:02:34.967 --> 00:02:38.467
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:45.371
Visal, a young engineer at our firm is talented but keeps missing deadlines. What would you recommend?

48
00:02:46.483 --> 00:02:52.662
I'd suggest that she seek a mentor rather than just attend another time-management course.

49
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.017
Why a mentor rather than a coach?

50
00:02:57.117 --> 00:03:09.080
A coach focuses on specific skills, whereas a mentor shares broader experience. I'd also recommend that the mentor be someone outside her direct reporting line.

51
00:03:10.183 --> 00:03:11.451
Why does that matter?

52
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:20.729
So she can admit difficulties freely. It's essential that the relationship remain confidential, otherwise trust never develops.

53
00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:23.541
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:27.904
Grammar focus: Subjunctive after suggest and recommend.

55
00:03:28.204 --> 00:03:29.425
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.117 --> 00:03:32.093
I suggest that she seek a mentor.

57
00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:36.237
We recommend that the mentor be outside her reporting line.

58
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.287
It is essential that the relationship remain confidential.

59
00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:52.837
After suggest, recommend, propose, insist, demand and ask (that), formal English uses the base verb for every subject: that he seek, that she be, that it remain. Negative: that he not be late.

60
00:03:53.583 --> 00:03:57.339
Be careful! Don't say: I suggest that she seeks a mentor.

61
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.414
Say: I suggest that she seek a mentor.

62
00:04:02.714 --> 00:04:04.586
Subjunctive: base verb.

63
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.099
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:09.959
Guiding Growth.

65
00:04:10.259 --> 00:04:15.881
Coaching and mentoring are often confused, yet they serve different purposes in professional development.

66
00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:25.287
Coaching is typically short-term and performance-focused, whereas mentoring is a longer relationship centred on overall growth and career direction.

67
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:33.350
Experts recommend that mentoring relationships be built on clear expectations, confidentiality and mutual commitment.

68
00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:40.408
Effective mentors ask more than they tell, encouraging reflection rather than providing ready-made answers.

69
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:50.551
Research suggests that people with mentors are promoted more often and report greater job satisfaction, benefits that frequently extend to the mentors themselves.

70
00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:55.819
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:56.683 --> 00:04:59.229
The doctor recommended that he blank more water.

72
00:04:59.529 --> 00:05:02.532
A, drinks. B, drink. C, drank.

73
00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:08.206
The answer is B: drink.

74
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.185
A person who is guided by a mentor is a blank.

75
00:05:12.485 --> 00:05:16.046
A, mentee. B, coach. C, mentor.

76
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.391
The answer is A: mentee.

77
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:25.293
Careful thought about one's experiences is blank.

78
00:05:25.593 --> 00:05:29.292
A, potential. B, feedback. C, reflection.

79
00:05:32.833 --> 00:05:35.286
The answer is C: reflection.

80
00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.450
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:45.956
A, She insisted that he is present.. B, She insisted that he be present.. C, She insisted that he to be present..

82
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.463
The answer is B: She insisted that he be present..

83
00:05:53.617 --> 00:05:56.487
According to the reading, effective mentors…

84
00:05:56.787 --> 00:06:03.529
A, give ready-made answers. B, ask more than they tell. C, focus only on short-term tasks.

85
00:06:07.067 --> 00:06:09.832
The answer is B: ask more than they tell.

86
00:06:11.033 --> 00:06:14.452
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:15.317 --> 00:06:17.630
Who has mentored you, and what did you learn?

88
00:06:34.417 --> 00:06:35.929
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.229 --> 00:07:09.002
My most influential mentor was my secondary-school English teacher, who noticed that I enjoyed reading but lacked confidence in speaking. Rather than simply correcting my mistakes, she suggested that I join the debate club and asked me to reflect on each performance. From her I learned that growth requires discomfort and that good feedback is specific and kind. Today, I recommend that every student find someone who believes in their potential before they believe in it themselves.

90
00:07:09.302 --> 00:07:11.221
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:12.117 --> 00:07:16.975
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and subjunctive after suggest and recommend.

92
00:07:17.275 --> 00:07:21.170
Today's value is mentorship. Share what you know to help others grow.

93
00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.029
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:07:24.329 --> 00:07:27.840
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

